e, showing that the ticket has been resolved and the resolution notes are available. The CoreID Admin Console menu is also visible, providing access to various features and settings.\n\n**EXTRACTED TEXT**\n\n*   Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution\n*   Status: Resolved\n*   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   CoreID Admin Console\n*   Dashboard\n*   Tickets\n*   Users\n*   Reports\n*   Settings\n\n**ERRORS AND WARNINGS**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY**\n\n*   Category: Software/application\n*   Severity: Low\n*   Reason: The ticket has been resolved, indicating that the issue was likely minor and has been addressed.\n\n**OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE**\n\n*   **OBSERVED FACTS**\n    *   The ticket has been resolved.\n    *   The resolution notes are available.\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console menu is visible.\n*   **INFERENCE**\n    *   The issue was likely minor and has been addressed.\n    *   The temporary password issued via CoreID is likely a security measure to prevent unauthorized access.\n\n**CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, making it easy to read the text and understand the context.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your ow